#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8f3989e-bde0-5b3a-8d45-08f34d330c3f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals of Increasing or Decreasing Displacement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d20b8dd4-3ddd-55b3-86fb-83375e88024f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313226} {:answer/id #uuid "a41cbfb8-13e7-5a6a-a618-8f4d2b734f6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313227} {:answer/id #uuid "82692d2c-fd1e-5b01-b3cb-6c8a0df337ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313228} {:answer/id #uuid "e2935a20-1892-5012-9267-697c870c8e06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313229} {:answer/id #uuid "327e9d84-65fe-5e64-abf1-9e0e1ec85e54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{2}{e})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a41cbfb8-13e7-5a6a-a618-8f4d2b734f6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313227} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bdadce8-c65a-5ba9-962b-2b2deff8f5d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "50f47d99-0d29-59c2-bb2e-982faa827891" :question/math-academy-id "q-109289" :question/problem "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t)=\\sqrt{t}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,$ where $t>0$ is the time measured in seconds. Determine the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x.$" :question/worked-solution "If the particle moves in the direction of decreasing $x,$ then its velocity is negative. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{2\\sqrt{t}}+\\frac{\\sqrt{t}}{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{2\\sqrt{t}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$, we solve the inequality $v(t)<0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & <0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2}{2\\sqrt{t}} & <0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2 & <0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡t & <-2 \\\\ t & <{e}^{-2} \\\\ t & <\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of decreasing $x$ is $t\\in (0,\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313231 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8f3989e-bde0-5b3a-8d45-08f34d330c3f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals of Increasing or Decreasing Displacement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82e30da0-c514-5d6b-a4f2-32b57f53eeab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313233} {:answer/id #uuid "feb1851d-1cdc-5e66-b8be-5e49f929eb6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313234} {:answer/id #uuid "13ed8951-f25a-51bc-80b8-2224b159127c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313235} {:answer/id #uuid "c3741a95-9af5-5d6c-82c3-c2495ede1033" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{1}{e})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313236} {:answer/id #uuid "0b552bf9-1beb-5221-a168-63e371ef621b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b552bf9-1beb-5221-a168-63e371ef621b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313237} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b305db0-a24f-59f4-9972-7b09dd35b380" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2912d1ed-8c93-531d-8d23-8555cf0ff32d" :question/math-academy-id "q-109290" :question/problem "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t)=t{e}^{-t},$ where $t>0$ is the time measured in seconds. Determine the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x.$" :question/worked-solution "If the particle moves in the direction of decreasing $x,$ then its velocity is negative. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}-t{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =(1-t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$, we solve the inequality $v(t)<0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & <0 \\\\ (1-t){e}^{-t} & <0 \\\\ 1-t & <0 \\\\ t & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of decreasing $x$ is $t\\in (1,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313250 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bee6d4bf-79da-5eb3-a214-8c045dbce5bd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle at the Moment When Its Acceleration is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8155132e-fb38-5ac5-a729-ddd6db4a43fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{2},\\,v=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313252} {:answer/id #uuid "69b7e53b-44b6-5593-8a6e-deb2504add7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{2},\\,v=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{e}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313253} {:answer/id #uuid "df7e3861-6845-507f-a3fe-2ab1edef4223" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2,\\,v=-\\frac{2}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313254} {:answer/id #uuid "38f0227d-9324-56e2-bff3-5e9ab523f2f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1,\\,v=\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313255} {:answer/id #uuid "4b9e018a-54ab-5d05-8046-62e2477ef1c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1,\\,v=-\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b9e018a-54ab-5d05-8046-62e2477ef1c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1,\\,v=-\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313256} :answer-field/id #uuid "bee01c62-f6b0-5470-ac4f-67bfcd38591e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313251}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f742d5d3-d314-55a4-a2e7-77b78815a252" :question/math-academy-id "q-73335" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=(t+1){e}^{-t},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t+1){e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =-t{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =(t-1){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(t-1){e}^{-t} \\\\ t-1 & =0 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t=1.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\nv(1)=-1\\cdot {e}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{e}.\n$$"}]}
